## Previous parts
- [Part 1](/blog/simple-guix)
- [Part 2](/blog/simple-guix2)

So after my foray into guix I went and tried Nix to purely benifit from the faster download speeds and more
frequent releases. My idea was that Nix may be fast enough that I could use it comfertably in VMs and
setup new systems. Unfortunately that didn't work out that way.

## My nix experience(s)
Now to be honest this isn't my first experience with Nix/NixOS. I've messed around with NixOS before and while
I was impressed the difficulty in figuring out how to use pins/lock files without flakes for even the basic
system `configuration.nix` made me give up. For me what I was trying to was pretty basic, but somehow even
multiple hours of googling didn't get me there. 

So this time I decided to take the flake pill, just accept the compromises and try to work with them.
After following a [great tutorial](https://github.com/Evertras/simple-homemanager) by Evertras. I was
able to get my [bootstrappable setup](https://github.com/kyserd/nix-home-manager-example) example in
Nix using home-manager and I was content.

But I was using `nix-shell -p` to create a ephemeral shell and I either got a warning or read somewhere
that `nix-shell -p` wasn't the prefered solution and that one should use the more modern `nix shell nixpkgs#...`
instead. Now that seems simple... right? NO. Whenever I tried to use this new syntax I would get an error that
nixpkgs is not found in the current directory. No amount of googling, finagling with channels, or blindly trusting
the LLMs got me anywhere.

So I gave up... why? Because if `nix shell` is the "better" (it may not be don't shoot me)
then it should just work with minimal trial and error. But it didn't, so I gave up. On using Nix at least.

## Enter mise
Now to be transparent I didn't solely give up on Nix because of my `nix shell` curfuffle. I was planning to switch
over to Bazzite as my main desktop OS because I wanted to have a non-frustrating Linux gaming experience. After using
LACT to set my GPU and VRAM clockspeeds to high by default (which fixed the annoying screen seizure that my AMD graphics
cards like to cause), my experience has been incredibly positive. As far as I'm concerned Bazzite has saved me a lot
of time.

Now Universal Blue (Bazzite's parent project) doesn't support Nix, because it doesn't play nicely with SELinux. I
did not want to deal with the hassle of making my own custom Bazzite with Nix and Distrobox wasn't exactly the
right fit for what I had in-mind. But I stumbled around [these dotfiles](https://github.com/rio/dotfiles) by Rio
I saw a pretty impressive solution to the problem I wanted to address.

Now while I didn't end up copying the dotfiles
exactly, the use of mise along with a dotfiles manager to copy my setup around seemed like a great idea. Especially if
I could create a simple bootstrapping script (similiar to what I did for Guix/Nix) that used mise as my launching
point for installing everything else, including my dotfiles manager. 

After reading through mises documentation I was impressed with how thorough the design and features of mise were. I was
especially impressed by the attention to security enhancements and the ability to use lock files (they're currently an
experimental feature at the time of writting but I've had a great time using them so far). Now I can use my preferred
tools on basically any system and best of all... mise is fast.

If you're also looking to try to set up reproducible dev tooling for yourself, or just wanna try it out, I would recommend
mise. At least at the moment, maybe I'll end up writing a mise postmortum in a few months :^)
